A||||7||donalson||best year suspension geometry to build track auto-x car on?||||1||
Z||000000||donalson||09-09-2001||02:59 AM||||hey guys this is my 1st time posting here...yes i'm an mr2 owner...friends of PearlBlueSoul.<P>anyway...the fiero has always been a neat car to me...the mid-engine design (which is what drew me to the mr2) and teh fact that you can put HUGE engines in it...i've never been a big fan of v-8s but think it would be great fun to have a v-8 fiero...at least one day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>anyway i wanta keep my eyes out for a fiero if i ever catch a good deal...also wanta learn some things about them...so heres my question (finaly)<P>what year fiero had the best suspension geometry for track and auto-x use?...i don't if the geometry...i don't care which came w/ stiffer springs, better struts, fater sway bars...all that can be upgraded...and will need to be w/ the addition of a larger motor.<P>so can ya guys help me out?<P>thanks<BR>mark||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sootah||09-09-2001||03:04 AM||||The '88 came with a totally redesigned suspension package. Made specifically for the Fiero. All other years just had standard GM parts thrown together. An '88 would be my guess, but I am by no means an expert on, well, anything.<P>Just my 2¢<BR>-<B>Sootah</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||09-09-2001||11:09 AM||||Yes '88s have a better designed suspension.  As you mentioned there are upgrades for all but because of the uniqueness of the '88 there are also less parts in general.  I wouldn't let that stop you though as 'there's a will, there's a way'.  GL Welcome to the forum and hope you get your first fiero soon.   PS how heavily did you mod the Mr2?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||09-09-2001||03:57 PM||||Hey Mark!<BR>Get any year Fiero, and get the replacement control arms made for racing...<BR>The more you mod the suspention, the less difference between the years...<BR>The 84-87 parts are easier to get too!<BR>If you are serious about racing, go with a 6, as the 8 is too squirly...<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||donalson||09-09-2001||05:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>Hey Mark!<BR>Get any year Fiero, and get the replacement control arms made for racing...<BR>The more you mod the suspention, the less difference between the years...<BR>The 84-87 parts are easier to get too!<BR>If you are serious about racing, go with a 6, as the 8 is too squirly...<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>by chance do any I6's fit in teh fiero?...longitudily maybe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe...sorry i'm a sucker for an I6...and the longitudaly mounted engine looks so nice.<P>what year fieros are the lightest?...didn't they only do 4spd trannys up till the '88's?<P>i'm kinda picky on my tranny it's gota be manual...how do the 4 and 5spd gear boxes compare gear wize mainly.<P><BR>and as for my mr2...it's 100% bone stock...i'm replacing the clutch w/ a toyota OEM right now (well have been and will finish today)...it'll be running in C-Stock auto-x when it's finaly fixed...but i have high hopes of getting a nicely worked mrI mr2 in the next few months...j-spec engine w/ worked head (engine pumping aprox 160 to the wheels)...after suspension it'll have cams, underdrive pulley, ignition, and individual throttle bodies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> the car has been lightend...only to be lightend even more when i get it...the suspension has been tuned mildley...and will be massivly tuned when i get it (money permiting).<P>it'll be a fun car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">...but i'd like to add to the mid-engine garrage as i have time and $$$ hehe<P>mark||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||09-09-2001||05:48 PM||||Hi Mark, welcome to the fourm!!  I love anything and everything mid-engine, as do most of us here I think you'll find!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The best year for the Fiero in my expierence is hands down the 1988. (any 88 has the same geometry, so you can get a car with a 4-cyl engine &lt;the fiero's 4-cyls suck, don't plan on racing them&gt; ) <P>The suspension, AND brake system was substantially upgraded in 1988, though at autocross speeds, the pre-88 brakes (if working properly) should be plenty fine.<P>Some people have had great success in swaping the entire rear engine cradle out of an 88 (this brings the entire suspension and rear brakes)  into an earlier year car.   They even say this makes for the absolute best handling fiero.   As I've never driven such a hybrid, I can't comment (I recomend you talk to "George Ryan" he has lots of expierence in that area.)<P>That individual throttlebody car sounds awesome, I *will* put velocity stacks on my car someday I tell you!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ps: check this out:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/northstar/northstar1.jpg">  4600cc 32valve all aluminum V8 with Fiero tranny going into my Silver car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||862M4inCA||09-10-2001||04:45 AM||||As for the question about the gearboxes, the 84 (and 85?) model(s) did not have the 5 sp. option, but all other years did. Currently I have the 5sp. in my 86 4 Cyl. and at highway speed (65-70) I'm generally running in the 22-2500 range. It jumps a bit so it's kinda hard to tell but I know for a fact at 55 it's solid on the 2K mark. as for the suspension question I would definately say that the 88 (according to hundreds of stories I've heard) is the most desired model based on the improved handling characteristics, but again, being the last year made makes replacement parts difficult and expensive to find. I would however try to steer you away from longitudinal placement of the engine since the whole car was designed around the transverse placement of the engine. The only time I could even think that longitude placement wouldn't have much adverse effect is if the engine remained slightly in front of the drive wheels, definately wouldn't want to make this car "rear engine". just some food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Will||09-10-2001||11:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>Hey Mark!<BR>Get any year Fiero, and get the replacement control arms made for racing...<BR>The more you mod the suspention, the less difference between the years...<BR>The 84-87 parts are easier to get too!<BR>If you are serious about racing, go with a 6, as the 8 is too squirly...<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There is absolutely no replacement for good geometry.<BR>Ever pushed an '88 hard? It doesn't sound like it.<BR>I wouldn't call an 8 "squirly", it just needs a different suspension setup and different driving style.<BR>And if you get the right 8, there's no difference except the power.||||reg||1
